What is Consensus in Blockchain?

Consensus is the process by which all the computers (nodes) in a blockchain network agree on what transactions are valid and what the current state of the blockchain should be.

Think of it like a group decision:

🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Many people have copies of the same notebook.
Before adding a new page, everyone needs a way to agree that the information on that page is correct.

In blockchain, consensus mechanisms provide that way.

Why is consensus needed?

Blockchain is decentralized, meaning there is no single central authority like a bank deciding:

Which transactions are valid
Who owns what
Which block should be added next
Whether someone is trying to spend the same funds twice

Consensus helps the network agree without requiring a central authority.

Common consensus mechanisms
Consensus	How it works	Example
Proof of Work (PoW)	Computers solve computational puzzles	Bitcoin
Proof of Stake (PoS)	Validators stake cryptocurrency to participate	Ethereum
Delegated Proof of Stake (DPoS)	Users delegate their stake to selected validators	Some PoS networks
Proof of Authority (PoA)	Approved validators create/validate blocks	Private/permissioned chains
Example: Proof of Stake

Suppose 100 validators are participating.
A user sends 10 ETH to another user.
The transaction is broadcast to the network.
A validator proposes a block containing the transaction.
Other validators check the block.
If the required consensus is reached, the block becomes part of the blockchain.
The blockchain is updated across the network.

So, in one line:

Consensus = the method a decentralized blockchain uses to make its participants agree on the valid state of the network.

Important: Consensus is not the same as encryption. Encryption protects information, while consensus helps the network agree on what is true/valid.

