##When does a validator in Avalanche finalize its decision?
anss 
After the preference is confirmed by the α-majority for β (Decision Threshold) consecutive rounds.
Correct
In Avalanche, a validator finalizes its decision after its preference is confirmed by the α-majority for β (Decision Threshold) consecutive rounds. It's not a single round process, it doesn't happen
immediately when a conflict arises, and it doesn't require replies from all validators in the system.

##What is the role of validators in the event of conflicting transactions?
ans 

Validators collectively decide on which of the two conflicting transactions will be accepted by all validators and determine the next state.
ans
In the event of conflicting transactions, validators have to collectively decide on which of the two conflicting transactions will be accepted by all validators and determine the next state. They do not act based on personal benefits, they don't 
reject all conflicting transactions, and they don't create new transactions to resolve conflicts.

#What happens during a single round of querying in the Avalanche Consensus protocol?
ans
A validator randomly selects a small subset of validators and asks for their current preference
In each round of Avalanche Consensus, a validator randomly selects a small subset (sample) of other validators and queries them for their 
current preference. This is more efficient than broadcasting to the entire network.
